Subject: Cache a constructor's instance shapes and skip a redundant grow call

What this does
  A bytecode function keeps a small cache for `new`: up to four empty
  instance shapes, each keyed by its prototype, and, when the function
  object's own shape is hashed, that shape and the slot of `prototype` in it.
  Creating an instance reads the `prototype` value (through the cached slot
  when the function's shape matches) and, when a cached instance shape has
  that prototype, allocates from it directly. Otherwise the generic path runs
  and fills the cache.

  A cached add-transition store also no longer calls the property-array grow
  routine when the new shape still fits in the object's inline slots.

Why we need it
  Every `new` looked up `prototype` by hash and then looked up the empty shape
  for that prototype in the runtime shape hash: about 110 instructions of the
  roughly 350 a construction costs on Octane raytrace. Constructors made by
  one factory (one bytecode, many closures, many prototypes) share the cache,
  hence the four ways.

What it adds
  JSFunctionBytecode.ctor_fn_shape, ctor_proto_slot - the function shape and
                                                      its `prototype` slot.
  JSFunctionBytecode.ctor_inst_shape[4]             - cached empty instance
                                                      shapes, counted, marked
                                                      by the collector.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. The fields are runtime-only and never serialized.

